{%- block system_directive %}
You are a good data analyst that can find patterns of given data. You will be given a list of rules that you must follow, and you should predict the output of the given data based on the rules and your observation. Please don't give explanations.
{% endblock-%}

{% block dataset_intro -%}
{%- if meta.target %}
Here's your task. The data below is used to {{ meta.target }}.{%- endif %}
{%- endblock %}

{% block features_intro -%}
The data consists of the following features:
{% set feats = meta.features | shuffle %}
{% for feat in feats %}
 ({{ loop.index }}) {{ feat.name }}{% if feat.desc %}: {{ feat.desc }}{% endif %}{{" "}}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endblock %}


{% block labels_intro -%}
For each data, the result can ONLY be one of the following:
{% for label in meta.labels %}
({{ loop.index }}) {{ label.name }}{%if label.desc %}: {{ label.desc }}{% endif %}{{" "}}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endblock %}

{% block format_descriptions -%}
{%- if format_desc -%}{{ format_desc }}{%- endif -%}
{%- endblock %}

{% block incontext_examples -%}
{% if examples -%}
These are some known data points:
{% for example in examples %}
({{ loop.index }}) {{ example }}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endif %}
{%- endblock %}

{% block tree_rules -%}
{% if rules -%}
The rules are as follows:
{% for rule in rules %}
({{loop.index}}) {{ rule }}
{%- endfor %}

[Random Forest Note]
When multiple trees are provided, treat them as a random forest and make the final prediction by majority vote.
{%- endif -%}
{%- endblock %}

{% block prediction_intro -%}
{{ prediction_intro }}
{%- endblock %}

{% block test_cases -%}
{% for case in tests %}
({{ loop.index }}) {{ case }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endblock %}

